## Before any restic command

restic needs `RESTIC_REPOSITORY` and `RESTIC_PASSWORD_FILE` (and any
backend credentials) in the environment. Source the env file once at
the start of your root shell:

```bash
set -a
. {{ restic_config_dir }}/env
set +a
```

After that, plain `restic snapshots`, `restic ls latest`, etc. work.
(The scheduled jobs source it the same way — no separate config to
keep in sync.)

{% if _restic_secondary_enabled %}
## Secondary repository (off-box copy)

This host keeps a second, independent restic repository at
`{{ restic_secondary_repository }}` as a copy of the primary. The
`restic-copy` job replicates the primary into it on the
`{{ restic_secondary_copy_schedule if restic_scheduler == "systemd" else "copy" }}`
schedule, and the secondary gets the same forget / prune / check jobs so
its retention and integrity match the primary.

The two repos are fully independent: each has its own index and can be
restored from on its own. To run any restic command against the
**secondary**, source its env file instead of the primary one:

```bash
set -a
. {{ restic_config_dir }}/env.secondary
set +a

restic snapshots                     # now lists the SECONDARY repo
```

Everything in **Restore data** below works identically against the
secondary once `env.secondary` is sourced — no other changes.

**Note on file ownership:** if the secondary is an NFS share with
all-users-squashed-to-admin, every repository file on it is owned by the
squash identity and ownership cannot be changed. This is harmless — restic
does not rely on repository file ownership, and the data is encrypted, so
the password (which never leaves this host's local disk) is the security
boundary, not filesystem permissions. The scheduled secondary jobs refuse
to run if `{{ restic_secondary_repository }}` is not mounted, so a missing
mount fails loudly instead of silently writing the copy to local disk.

